  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1145 AM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThis morning's upper air analysis featured a southern stream mid-\nlevel trough moving through the Lower Mississippi Valley.\nRegional WSR-88D radar trends showed some lingering rain showers\nmainly over north Mississippi late this morning, with other areas\nmostly rain free at this time. Temperatures ranged from the\nmiddle 50s to the lower 60s.\n\nRain showers will continue to diminish into this afternoon as the\naforementioned shortwave trough departs the Mid-South and\nshortwave ridging builds into the area. Highs this afternoon will\nrange from the lower 70s across portions of east Arkansas and\nnorthwest Mississippi to the lower 60s over northwest Tennessee.\nRain free weather will continue through Monday night across the\narea with temperatures rising back into the 70s into Tuesday as\nwarm air advection returns to the region.\n\nShort-term model trends continue to show the Pacific Coast upper-\nlevel trough ejecting into the Central and Southern Plains by\nMonday night and deepening as it moves through the Mississippi\nValley Tuesday night into Wednesday. Model consensus still shows\nthe best timing for onset of showers and thunderstorms across the\nMid-South occurring late Tuesday afternoon west of the\nMississippi River, then spreading east across the remainder of\nthe area Tuesday night. Despite favorable shear, mid-level height\nfalls, and upper-level forcing present, a lack of quality\ninstability is expected to limit the overall strong to severe\nthunderstorm potential as a cold front moves through the area and\ninstability decreases with the loss of diurnal heating. Much of\nthe model guidance including the RRFS shows instability being\npinched off as the system occludes across the region. LREF\nprobabilities of surface-based CAPE values of at least 500 J/kg,\nand 30 kts of shear remains at 10-15% at best over portions of\neast Arkansas south of I-40 and portions of northwest\nMississippi. NBM probabilities for 48 hour rainfall show a high\nchance (70-100%) for most locations receiving around an inch of\nrain, and a medium chance (40-60%) for some areas east of the\nMississippi River receiving at least 2 inches.\n\nHigh pressure will gradually build back into the Mid-South\nWednesday through Thursday night with long range ensembles\nshowing an additional upper-level trough and secondary cold front\nrotating through the region. Temperatures behind this secondary\nfront will be much cooler with highs mainly in the 60s next\nweekend with lows in the upper 30s to 40s.",
